1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,400
The Power of Meditation.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:09,675
Neuroimaging research has demonstrated that regular meditation produces measurable changes in brain structure and function.

3
00:00:09,675 --> 00:00:26,012
A landmark structural MRI study by Lazar et al. (2005) at Harvard Medical School, published in NeuroReport, found that experienced meditators had increased cortical thickness in the prefrontal cortex and right anterior insula compared to matched controls.

4
00:00:26,012 --> 00:00:36,187
Subsequent diffusion tensor imaging studies have shown increased white matter integrity in the anterior cingulate cortex, a region central to attention regulation.

5
00:00:36,187 --> 00:00:47,700
Functional MRI research reveals that mindfulness meditation reduces activity in the default mode network (DMN), the brain network associated with mind-wandering and self-referential thought.

6
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:54,912
Reduced DMN activity correlates with decreased rumination, a core feature of depression and anxiety disorders.

7
00:00:54,912 --> 00:01:12,050
The amygdala, a structure central to fear and stress responses, shows reduced gray matter volume and decreased functional reactivity after eight weeks of mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR), as documented in Social Cognitive and Affective Neuroscience (2013).

8
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:22,362
Electroencephalography studies demonstrate increased alpha and theta oscillations during meditation, patterns associated with relaxed alertness and sustained attention.

9
00:01:22,362 --> 00:01:40,937
Meta-analyses in JAMA Internal Medicine (2014) and The Lancet Psychiatry (2021) confirm that mindfulness-based interventions reduce symptoms of anxiety, depression, and chronic pain, with effect sizes comparable to first-line therapies for mild-to-moderate conditions.

10
00:01:40,937 --> 00:01:48,612
The neuroplastic effects appear dose-dependent: more practice hours correlate with greater structural and functional changes.

11
00:01:48,612 --> 00:02:04,987
Distinct meditation styles -- focused attention, open monitoring, and loving-kindness meditation -- engage partially overlapping neural circuits, with focused attention strengthening prefrontal attentional networks and open monitoring enhancing insular and somatosensory processing.

